WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.181
Security levels express increasing degrees of protection for industrial automation and control functions.

00:00:06.181 --> 00:00:11.509
They provide a structured way to translate risk into security expectations.

00:00:11.509 --> 00:00:21.527
A higher level represents resistance to a more capable, motivated, and well-resourced threat, but it also requires stronger capabilities, implementation, and evidence.

00:00:21.527 --> 00:00:28.987
The target security level describes the protection required for a zone or conduit based on risk.

00:00:28.987 --> 00:00:35.594
It should result from analysis of threats, consequences, exposure, existing safeguards, and risk tolerance.

00:00:35.594 --> 00:00:43.907
It is not selected simply because a system is important or because a supplier markets a high level.

00:00:43.907 --> 00:00:51.793
A component's capability security level describes the security capability it can support when correctly configured and used.

00:00:51.793 --> 00:00:57.974
The achieved security level describes what the integrated and operated system actually provides.

00:00:57.974 --> 00:01:01.597
These concepts are related but not interchangeable.

00:01:01.597 --> 00:01:09.483
A capable device can be undermined by poor architecture, shared credentials, weak remote access, or disabled features.

00:01:09.483 --> 00:01:16.091
Security level selection should be specific to the foundational requirements and the system context.

00:01:16.091 --> 00:01:23.550
A zone may need particularly strong integrity and restricted data flow while having different confidentiality needs.

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:32.289
Independent safety functions, physical controls, and operational procedures also influence risk, but they should be documented rather than assumed.

00:01:32.289 --> 00:01:33.781
Verification matters.

00:01:33.781 --> 00:01:44.225
Teams should be able to trace a target level to risk decisions, requirements, design controls, component capabilities, configuration, test results, and operational procedures.

00:01:44.225 --> 00:01:49.980
Changes to connectivity, process consequence, threat conditions, or components may require reassessment.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:54.029
Security levels are therefore decision and engineering tools.

00:01:54.029 --> 00:02:04.473
They do not certify an entire facility with one number, and they do not guarantee that a system remains secure throughout its life.
